# Services

Functions in `services.py` are named `<entity>_<action>`, take keyword-only arguments, and have type hints. Build the instance, call `full_clean()`, then `save()`. Prefer a function. Use a class only as a namespace for a multi-step flow. Updates go through an update service: set plain fields, then handle side effects. Services may call other services.

```python
# ❌ BAD — positional arguments, no full_clean, side effect first
def create_user(email, name):
    notify_user(email)
    return User.objects.create(email=email, name=name)

# ✅ GOOD
def user_create(*, email: str, name: str) -> User:
    user = User(email=email, name=name)
    user.full_clean()
    user.save()
    return user


def user_update(*, user: User, name: str) -> User:
    user.name = name
    user.full_clean()
    user.save(update_fields=["name", "updated_at"])
    notify_user(user.email)
    return user
```
